      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for sec+ and i'm kinda stuck on one thing. if the password is already being hashed, why do we even need to salt it? wouldnt the hash already make it secure anyway?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, my dear friend, even the most fortified castle walls can be breached if the guards are predictable. Salting a password before hashing it is like adding a secret ingredient to your stew. It makes the taste unique, even if someone else tries to replicate it. Without a unique salt, two identical passwords would produce the same hash, making it easier for a clever thief to guess the password by trying different combinations. So, while hashing is like turning your password into a secret code, salting is like adding a secret spice to that code, making it even harder for the goblins to crack it.",
