    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "goblins? lol what do you mean by that. also i'm not really getting the stew thing, can you explain the actual technical side of how it stops rainbow table attacks specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, my apologies for the goblin misstep! Let's dive into the stew of technicalities then. When you hash a password without salting, it's like having a recipe that everyone knows. If someone gets hold of the hash, they can use a rainbow table, which is a precomputed table for reversing cryptographic hash functions, to quickly find the original password.\n\nSalting a password is like adding a pinch of rare, secret herbs to your stew. Technically, a salt is a random value that you add to the password before hashing it. This means that even if two users have the same password, their salted hashes will be different because of the unique salt. When it comes to rainbow tables, each salt would require a separate table to be created, which is impractical due to the immense amount of storage and time needed. So, by salting, you make rainbow table attacks much less effective, as the attacker would need to generate a new table for each possible salt, which is like trying to make a new stew for every single diner in the kingdom. It's a lot of work, and it keeps those goblins at bay!",
      "source": "target",
